# Credentials — never commit.
#
# rsa_key* and credentials* are anchored to the repository root. Unanchored they
# also match DIRECTORY names, so data/client_c/credentials/Orders.csv was
# swallowed whole — git does not descend into an excluded directory, and no
# re-inclusion can rescue what is inside one. Under data/, credential-shaped
# names are caught loudly by scripts/check-data-integrity.sh instead; everywhere
# else the extension rules below still apply at any depth.
*.p8
*.pem
*.key
config.toml
.env
.env.*
/rsa_key*
/credentials*

# Snowflake CLI local state. Both filenames are listed explicitly because the
# directory rule is anchored to the repo root (so it cannot swallow a delivery),
# which leaves a nested .snowflake/ unprotected — and SNOWFLAKE_HOME can point
# anywhere. connections.toml is what the modern `snow` CLI stores credentials in.
/.snowflake/
connections.toml

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Editors
/.vscode/
/.idea/
*.swp

# Temporary exports
/tmp/
*.tmp

# ── data/ ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Nothing under data/ is ignored by NAME. Only extensions that are never a
# delivered record are ignored here.
#
# WHY. Both the manifest and the coverage check derive from the git index, so a
# delivered file that `git add` silently skips is invisible to every integrity
# gate: it never reaches Snowflake while CI reports green and the documentation
# asserts the delivery is byte-identical.
#
# Guarding that with name patterns cannot work, because both mistakes are silent.
# Too broad and a delivery vanishes: `*token*` swallowed PaymentTokens.csv and,
# worse, everything under data/tokens/ — git does not descend into an excluded
# directory, so no re-inclusion can rescue what is inside one. Too narrow and
# secrets.json stayed committable while the comment promised full coverage. Nine
# review rounds moved that line back and forth, each fix breaking the other side.
#
# So the mechanism changed. Names are not guessed at here at all. A credential
# carrying a data extension is caught by scripts/check-data-integrity.sh, which
# FAILS THE BUILD loudly and lets a human decide. Failing loudly beats ignoring
# silently: a false positive costs one conversation, a false negative costs a
# lost delivery or a published secret.
!data/**/*.csv
!data/**/*.CSV
!data/**/*.xml
!data/**/*.json
!data/**/*.txt

# Extensions that are never delivered data. Safe to ignore by extension, since
# no payload can hide behind one.
data/**/*.[Kk][Ee][Yy]
data/**/*.[Pp][Ee][Mm]
data/**/*.[Pp]8
data/**/.[Ee][Nn][Vv]*

# The directory patterns earlier in this file (/tmp/, /.snowflake/, /.vscode/,
# /.idea/) are anchored to the repository root on purpose: unanchored, `tmp/`
# swallowed data/client_c/tmp/Orders.csv and nothing noticed.
#
# scripts/check-data-integrity.sh asserts every property above — deliveries
# visible, non-data extensions ignored, no credential-shaped name tracked — and
# runs identically in CI and locally.
